course: technology
lesson: 36
title: Computers and screens
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A computer is not clever the way you are; it is incredibly fast and incredibly obedient. Its processor follows a list of exact instructions, called a program, doing billions of tiny steps every second, like a cook who reads a recipe one line at a time at lightning speed.
explain: Inside, everything is stored as ones and zeros, like millions of tiny switches that are either on or off. A photo, a song and your game save are all just very long rows of ones and zeros that the program knows how to read.
explain: The screen shows the results with millions of tiny dots called pixels. Each pixel has a red, a green and a blue part that glow a little or a lot, and mixing those three makes every colour you see, from a yellow banana to a purple dragon.
explain: All that work makes the chips hot, so a fan blows air over metal fins, which is why a laptop roars during a big game. And a password keeps your stuff yours: long, secret, and shared with no one except your parents.
fact: A computer has a processor that follows instructions very fast, memory that holds what it is working on and storage that keeps files when it is switched off; a modern processor does billions of simple steps every second.
fact: Computers store everything, every photo, song and game, as ones and zeros, called binary, like switches that are on or off.
fact: A computer program is a list of exact instructions written by programmers in a language the computer can follow; Ada Lovelace, in the 1840s, is often called the first computer programmer.
fact: An optical mouse has a light and a tiny camera underneath that take pictures of the desk many times a second to work out how it moved, so on clear glass with no pattern to see it gets lost; the first mouse, in the 1960s, was made of wood.
fact: A screen is made of millions of tiny dots called pixels, each with red, green and blue parts that light up in different amounts, and all three glowing brightly together look white; with a magnifying glass you can see them.
fact: Take breaks from screens and look into the distance now and then to rest your eyes.
fact: Chips get hot, so a fan blows air over metal fins to carry the heat away; never block the vents, and a laptop on a bed can overheat.
fact: A games console is a powerful computer made for games, with a special graphics chip that draws the whole picture many times every second; take breaks and keep game time balanced.
fact: An inkjet printer sprays tiny drops of ink, while a laser printer uses a laser to put powder on the paper and heat to fix it, which is why its pages come out warm.
fact: The cloud is really other people's computers in big data centres, reached through the internet, and data centres use huge amounts of electricity and cooling.
fact: A password is a secret that proves it is you, and computers store only a scrambled version of it; long phrases of random words can be stronger than short tricky ones.
fact: Never share a password with anyone except your parents.
exercise: What does a computer's processor do? | Follows instructions very fast | Keeps files when it is switched off | Shows the picture on the screen | Cools the computer down
exercise: About how many simple steps can a modern processor do every second? | Billions | About ten | About a hundred | Exactly one
exercise: Which part keeps your files when the computer is switched off? | Storage | Memory | The fan | The screen
exercise: How does a computer store a photo or a song? | As ones and zeros | As tiny printed pictures | As little recordings on tape | As letters of the alphabet
exercise: What is a computer program? | A list of exact instructions the computer follows | A TV show about computers | The plastic case of the computer | The electricity a computer uses
exercise: Who is often called the first computer programmer? | Ada Lovelace, in the 1840s | Alessandro Volta, back in 1800 | Henry Ford, the car maker, in 1913 | Percy Shaw, the cat's eye man, in 1934
exercise: How does an optical mouse know which way it moves? | A tiny camera underneath takes many pictures of the desk | A small ball inside rolls on the screen | It listens to the sound of your hand | It feels the desk getting warmer
exercise: What was the first computer mouse made of? | Wood | Glass | Gold | Rubber
exercise: What is a pixel? | One of the tiny dots that make up a screen | A small battery hidden in the corner of the screen | A word in a computer program | The cable that joins the screen
exercise: Which three colours do the parts of a pixel glow? | Red, green and blue | Red, yellow and blue | Black, white and grey | Orange, purple and green
exercise: Why does a laptop get noisy when you play a big game? | The fan spins faster to carry heat from the hot chips | The speakers turn up on their own | The screen hums when it is busy | The battery rattles about inside because it is shaken by the game's loud sounds
exercise: Why does a games console need a special graphics chip? | To draw the whole picture many times every second | To store all the games safely when the console is switched off | To play the music in games | To keep the controller charged
exercise: Why is the paper warm from a laser printer? | Heat is used to fix the powder onto the paper | The ink is boiled in a little pot before it is sprayed on | The paper is stored in a warm drawer | The laser burns the words into it
exercise: Where is the cloud, really? | Other people's computers in big data centres | In the real clouds up in the sky, as tiny drops of water | Inside your own computer's fan | On a satellite above every house
exercise: What makes a good password? | A long phrase of random words kept secret | Your name and your birthday together | The word password with a 1 at the end | A short word your friends can easily guess
exercise: How do computers store your password? | As a scrambled version | Written on a sticky note | Spoken out loud in the data centre | Shown on the screen to everyone
exercise: How can you rest your eyes during screen time? | Take breaks and look into the distance | Turn the screen as bright as it goes so your eyes work less | Sit as close as possible | Close one eye at a time
test: «my best mate wants my game password, he's my best mate so it's fine» What is the rule? | Never share a password, except with your parents | Share it with your best friend only | Share it if he shares his | Share it if you promise each other to change both of them next week
test: «doing homework on the laptop on my bed, it's dead hot, eh» What should you do? | Put it on a hard flat surface so the vents are not blocked | Put a pillow on it to cool it down | Keep going, laptops cannot overheat | Cover it with a blanket
test: In a movie, a computer gets clever by itself and decides to plan a party. What is true of real computers? | They follow programs written by people, step by step | They think and feel exactly like people, and they get bored and want to have fun | They decide what to do with no program | They learn only by watching TV
test: «my brother reckons the cloud is a real cloud and rain might wipe my photos» What is the best correction? | The cloud is computers in data centres, reached through the internet | Right, so save photos on sunny days | Right, the cloud lives above the house | The cloud is inside the phone's camera
test: You look at a white part of a screen through a magnifying glass. What do you see? | Tiny red, green and blue dots | Tiny white dots only | Tiny black and white squares | Nothing, the screen is smooth
test: What would happen if a computer's fan broke while you played a big game? | The chips would get too hot, and it might slow down or switch itself off | It would run faster because it is quieter | Nothing, fans are only for decoration | The screen would turn blue forever
test: Count them: in binary, each switch is on or off. How many different patterns can 2 switches make? | 4 | 2 | 3 | 8
test: Count them: a tiny screen picture is 4 pixels wide and 3 pixels tall. How many pixels? | 12 | 7 | 16 | 9
test: Count them: each pixel has 3 coloured parts. How many coloured parts in 5 pixels? | 15 | 8 | 10 | 20
test: «why do I need a long password, short ones are easier, duh» What is the best answer? | Long ones are much harder for strangers to guess | Short ones are harder to guess | Length does not matter at all | Long passwords slow the computer down every time you log in to a game
test: «mouse ain't working on the glass table, stupid thing» What is the likely reason? | Its camera cannot see any pattern on clear glass to track | Glass is too cold for mice | Mice only work on wooden desks and the glass makes the light go out | The mouse's ball fell out
exam: Identify the form in which a computer retains all data, including images and sound. | Sequences of ones and zeros, corresponding to switches in the on or off state | Miniature printed images | Recordings upon magnetic tape exclusively | Letters of the alphabet
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a computer program? | A list of exact instructions, composed by programmers, which the computer executes | A television broadcast concerning computers | The external casing of the apparatus | The electrical supply of the apparatus
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the location of data saved to the cloud. | It is retained upon computers housed in data centres and accessed through the internet | It is retained within clouds in the atmosphere | It is retained exclusively within the user's own device | It is retained upon a satellite positioned above each dwelling
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the fan of a computer accelerates during demanding tasks. | The chips generate additional heat which must be carried away | The loudspeakers are thereby activated | The screen requires additional air | The battery requires agitation in order to release further electrical energy
